<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luapula River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luapula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luapula is a river of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, part of the upper Congo system, that flows from the Bangweulu swamps north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a> and forms much of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Its fertile, fish-rich valley was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a> from about 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luapula-river.jpg" alt="Luapula River" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kwamikagami · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luvua_-_Luapula_-_Chambeshi_River_DRC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Luapula River flows north in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> watershed. It rises in the wetlands of Lake Bangweulu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, which the Chambeshi River feeds, flows west and then north, marks the border between Zambia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, and empties into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a>. It gives its name to Zambia's Luapula Province. Below the Bangweulu swamps it passes rapids and the Mambilima Falls, and at Tangwa it has cut caves, arches and potholes in rocky hills.</p><p>The explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> found the swamps a mass of shifting channels and lagoons. He died exploring the area, and one of his last acts was to question Chief Chitambo about the course of the Luapula. The river is part of the longest tributary of the Congo and so, by convention, part of its source.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Luapula Valley runs for 300 km from the Chembe Bridge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a> and holds a larger rural population than the plateau around it. The fisheries and fertile farmland of the lower valley attracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> invaders under Mwata Kazembe, whose capital is at Mwansabombwe. Arab traders and slavers from Zanzibar and Portuguese traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> came in the 18th and 19th centuries. Livingstone visited in 1867, and Mwata Kazembe told him that the Luapula links the Chambeshi and Bangweulu with the Mweru, Luvua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba</a> systems.</p><p>The Garanganze Mission of the Plymouth Brethren settled at Mambilima around 1892, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> at Mbereshi in 1900. Kasenga became the first large town of the colonial era and prospered in the 1930s by supplying fish to Elizabethville and the Katanga Copperbelt. Before roads, people travelled by canoe between lakes Bangweulu and Mweru, and during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> a fleet of 1,885 canoes and small boats carried matériel to Chambeshi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The swamps cover about 2,500 square kilometres along the last 100 km of the river, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> beds float in them. The main channel stays about 400 metres wide. The first bridge over the river, the Luapula Bridge on the Samfya-Serenje Road, was built in 1983, and the Chembe Bridge replaced the Chembe Ferry in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luapula River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the empire's height under Ilunga Sungu (c. 1780–1810), Kumwimbe Ngombe (c. 1810–1840) and Ilunga Kabale (c. 1840–1870), Luba expeditions pushed east towards Lake Tanganyika, north into Manyema and south towards the Kazembe kingdom of the <b>Luapula</b>, consolidating tributary networks and securing trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion in the late seventeenth and eighteenth centuries carried Lunda power into the Kasai region and towards Angola, while an eastern expedition founded the kingdom of Kazembe on the <b>Luapula River</b> around 1740, consolidating tributary states and securing trade corridors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1740 a Lunda general sent east by the Mwata Yamvo of the Lunda Empire conquered the <b>Luapula</b> valley south of Lake Mweru.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kazembe">Kingdom of Kazembe</a></li></ul></section>
